  "physics": {
    "note": "ONE SOURCE OF TRUTH. lib/orbit.tsx holds GM, R_EARTH and ISS_ALT, and EVERY number below is derived from them by the composition at render time — the chips, the ladder, the downrange labels and the equality panel all read from the same functions that draw the arcs. Nothing here is asserted; the arcs land (or don't) because velocity-Verlet integration under Newtonian gravity says so. Same ethos as prob.tsx's seeded Monty Carlo sim and map.tsx's real Mercator.",
    "constants": {
      "GM_m3_s2": 398600441800000.0,
      "R_earth_m": 6371000,
      "ISS_alt_m": 400000
    },
    "derived": {
      "g_surface_m_s2": 9.8203,
      "g_at_400km_m_s2": 8.6943,
      "pct_of_surface_gravity": 88.5,
      "v_circular_m_s": 7672.6,
      "period_min": 92.4,
      "fall_in_1s_m": 4.347,
      "curve_away_in_1s_m": 4.347,
      "sideways_in_1s_km": 7.67,
      "crossCheck": "our simulated period, 92.4 min, against the real ISS's ~92.7 min — the 0.3% gap is the ISS's slightly elliptical, 51.6-degree-inclined orbit and a non-spherical Earth. The sim is honest."
    },
    "THE_CORRECTION": {
      "claim_as_briefed": "'in 1 s the ISS falls 4.35 m; it travels 7.7 km sideways; over that distance the EARTH'S SURFACE curves away by 4.35 m'",
      "why_it_is_wrong": "the GROUND (r = 6371 km) is more sharply curved than the shell the station is on. Its sagitta over a 7.67 km chord is 4.62 m, not 4.35 m. Quoting 4.35 m as a fact about the ground is a 6% error, and the whole video rests on this number being EXACT.",
      "what_is_exactly_true": "the shell the station orbits on (r = R + h = 6771 km) drops 4.347 m over that same 7.67 km — identical, to every digit we show, to the 4.347 m it falls. And the shell IS what 'the Earth curving away beneath it' means for something at altitude: it is the surface of constant height above the ground, so keeping pace with it is exactly what keeps the station's altitude constant. That equality is not a coincidence to be measured — it IS the definition of a circular orbit, which is why it comes out exact.",
      "resolution": "sagitta() in lib/orbit.tsx takes the radius as an argument, and the composition passes r = R + h. The VO says 'the Earth curves away by exactly the same amount' and never names a second, different number, so the claim on the track equals the number on the screen."
    },
    "orbit_plane": {
      "note": "the orbit is drawn in the plane of the screen around a globe facing us near (12E, 18N) — geometrically that is a polar orbit. The real ISS is inclined 51.6 degrees, which is the same circle rotated: it changes nothing about the speed, the period, the fall per second or the curvature. We never claim an inclination."
    },
